  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Balakrishnan V vs Food Inspector on 14 January, 2016 Author: B.Sudheendra Kumar Bench: B.Sudheendra Kumar In The High Court Of Kerala At Ernakulam Present: The Honourable Mr. Justice B.Sudheendra Kumar Thursday, The 14Th Day Of January 2016/24Th Pousha, 1937 Crl.Rev.Pet.No. 2342 of 2013 () -------------------------------- Crl.A 4/2012 Of The Additional Sessions Court - Ii, Kasaragod Cc 694/2009 Of The Judicial First Class Magistrate Court-Ii, Hosdrug Revision Petitioner(S)/Appellant/1St Accused: -------------------------------------------- Balakrishnan V., Aged 55 Years S/O.Appu, Chithra Bakery Door No.Npw1/1015A Near Police Station, Nileshwar Nileshwar Village & Post, Hosdurg Taluk (Vadakkeyil House, Tayineri, Payyannur Kannur District.) By Advs.Sri.M.Ramesh Chander (Sr.) Sri.Aneesh Joseph Smt.Dennis Varghese Respondent(S)/Respondent/Complainant: ------------------------------------- 1. Food Inspector Kanhangad Circle, Kanhangad P.O. - 671 315 Kasaragod District. 2. State Of Kerala, Represented By Public Prosecutor High Court Of Kerala, Ernakulam - 682 031. R By Public Prosecutor Sri.R.Githesh Balakrishnan V vs Food Inspector on 14 January, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/71993604/ 1 This Criminal Revision Petition Having Been Finally Heard On 14-01-2016, The Court On The Same Day Passed The Following: Scl. B. Sudheendra Kumar, J. ------------------------------------- Crl.R.P. No.2342 of 2013 -------------------------------------- Dated this the 14th day of January, 2016 Order",
    "facts": "The first accused in C.C.No.694 of 2009 on the files of the Court of the Judicial Magistrate of First Class-II, Hosdurg, has filed this revision petition challenging the concurrent finding of conviction and sentence passed by the courts below under Section 16 (1) (a) (i) r/w Section 2 (ia) (m) and 7(i) of the Prevention of Food Adulteration Act r/w Rule 5 of Appendix-B item A.28.04 of the Prevention of Food Adulteration Rules and section 16 (1) (a) (ii) r/w 7 (iii) of the Prevention of Food Adulteration Act r/w Rule 50 of the Prevention of Food Adulteration Rules. The prosecution allegation can be briefly stated thus:- On 16.5.2009 at 10.30 a.m., PW1, the Food Inspector purchased 600 grams of broken cashew nuts from the first accused. The second accused was the distributor. After sampling as per rules, one sample was sent for analysis to the Public Analyst. Ext.P12 report of the Public Analyst would show that the sample did not conform to the standard prescribed for dry fruits and nuts under the Prevention of Food Adulteration Rules and was therefore adulterated. It was further found that the first accused had no licence to sell the food articles. 4. Before the trial Court, PW1 to PW5 were examined and Exts.P1 to P34 were marked for the prosecution. No evidence was adduced on the side of the revision petitioner.",
    "arguments": "5. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner has submitted that since no laboratory for the analysis of the food item was defined and no validated method of analysis was prescribed as per the mandate of Section 23 (1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act, Ext. P12 cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased by the Food Inspector was adulterated. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner relied on the decision of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (P) Ltd. v. Food Inspector [(2011) 1 SCC 176] to buttress his argument. It was held by the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) that since the Laboratories were not notified and the tests conducted by such laboratories were not admissible in evidence, no prosecution could be based on such report and the accused was entitled to get acquittal. In the decision of this Court in Gopalakrishnan v. Food Inspector [2013 (3) KLT 455], the court relied on the decision of the Supreme Court in Pepsico (supra) and found that the Public Analyst's report was not admissible in evidence and accordingly, the Court acquitted the accused.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "5. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner has submitted that since no laboratory for the analysis of the food item was defined and no validated method of analysis was prescribed as per the mandate of Section 23 (1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act, Ext. P12 cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased by the Food Inspector was adulterated. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner relied on the decision of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (P) Ltd. v. Food Inspector [(2011) 1 SCC 176] to buttress his argument.",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "It was held by the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) that since the Laboratories were not notified and the tests conducted by such laboratories were not admissible in evidence, no prosecution could be based on such report and the accused was entitled to get acquittal. In the decision of this Court in Gopalakrishnan v. Food Inspector [2013 (3) KLT 455], the court relied on the decision of the Supreme Court in Pepsico (supra) and found that the Public Analyst's report was not admissible in evidence and accordingly, the Court acquitted the accused."
  },
